From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 00:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] compat: skip __kernel_timespec when the kernel already has
 it

compat.h defines struct __kernel_timespec for anything older than 4.18,
going by LINUX_VERSION_CODE alone. LineageOS's 4.14.357 for the Pixel 4a
(sunfish) backports the time64 headers, and its linux/time64.h has

  #define __kernel_timespec timespec

so the compat definition expands to a second struct timespec and every
object fails:

  compat.h:789:8: error: redefinition of 'timespec'

Unlike the static inline functions in 0001 and 0002, this one is a
macro, so the preprocessor can see it: skip the definition when
__kernel_timespec is already defined. linux/time64.h is included first
so the test does not depend on what happened to be included before
(it exists from 3.17 on). Kernels that follow the version arithmetic are
unaffected: nothing defines the macro and the struct is defined as
before.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [upstream is archived; this is vendor-tree specific]
---
--- a/compat/compat.h
+++ b/compat/compat.h
@@ -785,10 +785,19 @@
 #define totalram_pages() totalram_pages
 #endif
 
+/* A kernel that backported the time64 headers - LineageOS's 4.14.357 for the
+ * Pixel 4a does - already has __kernel_timespec as a macro for timespec, so a
+ * definition here would be a second struct timespec.
+ */
 #if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(4, 18, 0)
+#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(3, 17, 0)
+#include <linux/time64.h>
+#endif
+#ifndef __kernel_timespec
 struct __kernel_timespec {
 	int64_t tv_sec, tv_nsec;
 };
+#endif
 #elif LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(5, 1, 0)
 #include <linux/time64.h>
 #ifdef __kernel_timespec
